Ladybird exposes a cross-origin child frame on its parent’s Window when
the child’s target name matches its container’s name attribute, and
replicates that attribute to processes that host the parent remotely.
Blink does the same: Its Window named getter in window_properties.cc
compares the name with the container name the child’s owner reports,
Owner()->BrowsingContextContainerName(), which a RemoteFrameOwner takes
from the replicated FrameOwnerProperties. WebKit looks a child up by the
name its owner element’s name attribute gave it, through FrameTree::
scopedChildBySpecifiedName(). And Gecko’s RemoteOuterWindowProxy exposes
any child whose browsing-context name matches, with no check against
the container’s name attribute at all.
